that maybe, maybe they're not anymore.

00:02:37.639 --> 00:02:43.639
And one of those, uh, sources of difficulty is responding to, uh, support issues when

00:02:43.639 --> 00:02:45.639
they get escalated to the engineering team.

00:02:45.759 --> 00:02:48.440
So Posit uses Zendesk for support.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.839
So customers can file tickets when they run into issues and the support

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:56.279
engineers will field them and usually they can, they can address them.

00:02:56.279 --> 00:03:00.559
But sometimes it's a harder thing to debug or, you know, a clear bug or

00:03:00.559 --> 00:03:03.759
something, and it gets sent to the engineering team and, you know, for an

00:03:03.759 --> 00:03:07.199
engineer to pick this up, you have to put down whatever they were already working

00:03:07.199 --> 00:03:12.279
on and try to reason about the particular environment that Connect is deployed in.

00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:15.639
It's on-prem software, so it could vary quite a bit by the customer.

00:03:15.679 --> 00:03:18.039
And that's, for humans, that's hard.

00:03:18.119 --> 00:03:21.759
Uh, but I thought, you know, Cloud's really good at reading through the code

00:03:21.759 --> 00:03:25.600
base, helping understanding how things work and even, you know, finding bugs.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.720
Like what if I could just point Cloud at Zendesk and say something like this,

00:03:29.839 --> 00:03:32.119
here, help me with this Zendesk ticket.

00:03:32.479 --> 00:03:33.199
This doesn't work.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.759
Cause Cloud will try to do this, but it will get a 403 or a 401 rather.

00:03:37.759 --> 00:03:38.720
You're not authenticated.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.440
So I can't see what's in that ticket.

00:03:40.639 --> 00:03:42.600
And it says, could you just paste the tickets in here?

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.720
I'm like, well, no, that's, that defeats the purpose.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.520
That's, that's the work I would want to do.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.919
And the first problem we ran into is, is this authentication?

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:55.119
And there's really not a way for me to provide my credentials to Cloud Code to do this.

00:03:55.119 --> 00:04:00.279
And even if I could, I wouldn't want my API key or my password in the context here.

00:04:00.559 --> 00:04:03.039
You know, Mac was talking about a number of security things that come up here.

00:04:03.039 --> 00:04:06.520
Like that's very sensitive data that I don't want circulating here.

00:04:06.639 --> 00:04:15.320
But even if I could do that, the Zendesk API is huge and is really not designed for AI agents to make both efficient and secure use of it.

00:04:15.559 --> 00:04:17.239
And this is what MCP is designed for.

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:22.000
So MCP provides a way for you to authenticate with your APIs without giving your credentials to the agent.

00:04:22.279 --> 00:04:26.920
And by writing custom MCP servers, you can control exactly what tools are made available.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.119
And this makes it more efficient and more secure.

00:04:29.399 --> 00:04:34.920
And by hosting your own MCP server, you have a way to easily distribute it and share it with your team.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:41.279
And a point that I want to make sure comes across here is that you can write and host your own MCP servers to do exactly what you need.

00:04:41.519 --> 00:04:45.320
Because as we know, code is very cheap to produce now.

00:04:45.359 --> 00:04:47.160
It doesn't require specialized knowledge.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:52.839
You basically just have to ask your agent to say, I would like an MCP server and, and you're off and running.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:59.880
And so my talk is really going to focus on writing your own and not about whether you should use some MCP server that's out there that someone else is providing.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:04.200
And even if you do want to use that, there's probably reasons why you might want to customize it anyway.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.679
I'm going to, there's a link here, I'll have a QR code at the end.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:15.600
If you're curious of the MCP server that I ended up making to talk to Zendesk to do the thing that I was trying to do at first and was frustrated by.

00:05:15.679 --> 00:05:21.239
I've said MCP a bunch of times, I should probably say what it is, MCP model context protocol.

00:05:21.279 --> 00:05:28.119
Uh, I've talked to a lot of people in a lot of different industries about this lately, and I've found a lot of confusion about what it is.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.519
And so I first want to say what MCP is not.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:34.559
It is not magic illusions.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.559
It's not AI.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.320
It's not vector search.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.119
It's not any of these things.

00:05:39.119 --> 00:05:42.600
You could put vector search behind an MCP server.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.959
You could put AI behind an MCP server.

00:05:45.399 --> 00:05:50.079
But MCP is really just a specification for how to provide tools to LLMs.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.279
So tools are functions that an LLM can call.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:59.720
And the definitions of these tools, uh, are in the kind of start of the context window that you have.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.880
And so a model will see that a function exists by this name.

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.359
Here's a description of what it does and what inputs it takes and that kind of thing.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:15.959
So MCP is a standard for, uh, developing servers that provide those tools and for client applications, any AI application to use them.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.640
I was talking about cloud code and that was the example I showed there, but it's not limited to code.

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:24.640
AI applications, any AI application that supports MCP can use MCP servers.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.440
They don't have to be about writing code.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:28.720
Uh, there's two kinds of MCP servers.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:33.079
There's local ones that run on your machine and remote ones that run on someone else's machine.

00:06:33.119 --> 00:06:44.399
As you can see from this, from that example of like what the command you would use to add an MCP, local MCP to cloud code, you know, it's calling UVX, could be NPX or something like that.

00:06:44.399 --> 00:06:46.320
You're running code locally.

00:06:46.799 --> 00:06:50.399
And often it's pointing at a GitHub repository or something.

00:06:50.559 --> 00:06:54.799
Because it's running locally, you can access things on your local system that can be really useful.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:07:03.839
It can also be kind of risky, particularly if you're going to some random GitHub repository and you're not pinning to a SHA that you're pulling there and it could be untrusted code.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:08.079
Uh, remote ones, remote ones run somewhere else and you just reference them by the URL.

00:07:08.239 --> 00:07:09.920
And so that's really nice for distribution.

00:07:10.239 --> 00:07:19.279
They can't access your local file system, but they could have access to other things that are maybe are available on the server they're running that aren't available to you locally.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:20.359
So there's some trade-offs there.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:23.720
I'm going to talk about, mostly about remote MCP servers.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:30.040
And because I think that the, the real, uh, value of MCP today really comes from remote MCP servers.

00:07:30.239 --> 00:07:37.920
But it also, it really helps with the mental model of what MCP is because MCP is basically just an API standard.

00:07:38.239 --> 00:07:41.480
A remote MCP server is communicating over HTTP.

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:42.880
It makes post requests.

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:43.720
It sends JSON.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:44.959
It gets JSON back.

00:07:45.079 --> 00:07:45.839
This is an API.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:53.239
And so, you know, for people that, you know, many of you in this room, you've worked with data, you've worked with APIs throughout your career.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.519
You know what APIs are, you know how to talk to them, you know how to make requests, get response.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:07:59.480
This is a familiar thing.

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:05.480
This is not anything other than a convention of how to structure API endpoints.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:06.720
So you know how to make these.

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:10.040
MCP was, uh, first announced in the end of 2024.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:11.119
Anthropic put it out.

00:08:11.119 --> 00:08:12.880
It's now under the Linux foundation.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:15.359
There's an industry consortium that's supporting it.

00:08:15.359 --> 00:08:27.480
When it came out, there was a lot of enthusiasm because, you know, there were things that you could do with these tools that there wasn't really a good standard way for providing external access to external resources before this.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:31.959
Perhaps not surprisingly, after a lot of exuberance, there was some backlash.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:37.039
One of them being that, why do I need to use, for example, GitHub put out an MCP server.

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:38.200
Why would I need that?

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:42.280
When cloud code already knows how to use the GH command line tool.

00:08:42.559 --> 00:08:43.679
This is just a waste.

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:46.200
Leading some to say that MCP is dead.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:46.960
I disagree.

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:53.760
Of course, I think, you know, as with many things, there's truth in both the initial excitement and the backlash against it.

00:08:53.799 --> 00:08:55.760
When it came out, there weren't great alternatives.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:06.960
People were doing things with MCP that maybe is not the best use of, um, and, but that doesn't mean there's not still important places where MCP fits in today.

00:09:07.039 --> 00:09:11.640
I'm gonna talk about a few of the critiques and where that I think shakes things out for MCP today.

00:09:11.679 --> 00:09:15.479
One line of questioning is what is the right tool for the job?

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:17.280
Is it an MCP server?

00:09:17.679 --> 00:09:20.039
Is it a command line tool or is it a skill?

00:09:20.239 --> 00:09:25.200
The agent skills standard came out, I guess it was 2025 after MCP.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:31.719
And so, you know, there are many things that you can effectively do just by writing some texts, markdown.

00:09:31.919 --> 00:09:39.440
And so if you can explain with existing tools that your agent already has, just how to use those tools, that's what skills are good for.

00:09:39.599 --> 00:09:44.080
Otherwise, skills are often compliments to MCP servers or CLIs.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:49.239
You provide tools and you also need to provide a description of how to use the tools, what you might use the tools for.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:57.039
So if we're trading, if we're discussing, if we're thinking MCP and skill versus CLI and skill, there's really a couple of places where CLIs are great.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.000
I mentioned the example of GitHub.

00:09:59.239 --> 00:10:03.320
So the gh command line utility has been around for a number of years.

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:05.359
It's in the training data, uh, in model.

00:10:05.359 --> 00:10:08.840
And so I can be in cloud code, cloud code knows how to use gh.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:10.119
I don't have to tell it how to do it.

00:10:10.119 --> 00:10:11.880
It doesn't need a bunch of tool definitions.

00:10:12.159 --> 00:10:16.960
It doesn't need to do gh dash dash help to see what the command line says it does.

00:10:17.159 --> 00:10:17.760
So that's great.

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:24.840
And so you save all of these, uh, tokens that the context window, the context that you would take up with these tool definitions.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:25.440
That's great.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:27.159
If that exists, it doesn't exist for everything.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:33.400
And so if you're the command line that you want to, utility that you want to use is not widely available, it's not in the training data.

00:10:33.599 --> 00:10:38.679
You're going to have to, either the model's going to have to discover by trying things, how to use it.

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:40.960
Or you're going to have to explain it in a skill.

00:10:41.119 --> 00:10:44.880
And now you've kind of, you've lost that savings of the tool definition.

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:53.400
Cause MCP servers, I should, I should clarify when you connect an MCP server, it pulls all of the, uh, tool definitions up front.

00:10:53.599 --> 00:10:59.760
And so the GitHub MCP server has dozens, maybe hundreds of MCP tools.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:02.200
If you were to use them all, that's a lot of tokens.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:03.320
And so that's what you're trying to save.

00:11:03.559 --> 00:11:09.840
The other place where a CLI is, the other thing you need, if you're going to use a CLI is you need to be able to run commands in the shell.

00:11:10.039 --> 00:11:11.760
And not every AI application has that.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:22.239
You might be building an agent, uh, an AI agent yourself, and you don't want to give it access to the, to bash because you want to keep it constrained to what its abilities are.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:25.919
Maybe you're using a desktop application that's not cloud code.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.239
And so CLIs don't have the same reach there that MCP does.

00:11:30.479 --> 00:11:34.400
There's also more things that you can do with a remote MCP server that you just can't do with a CLI.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.119
I mentioned authentication upfront.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:40.840
In the MCP standard, there is this OAuth flow that is specified.

00:11:40.919 --> 00:11:47.400
And what that means is you can register your, your MCP server with your application.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:53.080
And if the place you've deployed it supports this, it will take you to the, when you try to connect, it'll take you to the browser.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:55.599
You'll log in and it'll say authentication successful.

00:11:55.599 --> 00:11:57.840
You can close this tab and it handles it automatically.

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:01.239
So you don't have to have secrets sitting locally on your machine.

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:02.000
That's great.

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:03.400
And you just can't do that otherwise.

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:12.159
I mentioned before, you could have databases or things that you only want to expose within the server where you're running the MCP server, uh, that you wouldn't have access to locally.

00:12:12.159 --> 00:12:16.440
And this gives you a, an AI, a, an AI forward portal to those.

00:12:16.479 --> 00:12:24.400
Monitoring, uh, some of the earlier, uh, presentations we're talking about, you know, having the audit trail of things, these sorts of enterprising things.

00:12:24.799 --> 00:12:26.599
It's particularly important with AI.

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:29.599
And you can get these with a remote MCP server.

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:31.960
Speaking of security, it's a big deal.

00:12:32.119 --> 00:12:33.559
Mac talked a lot about that.

00:12:33.799 --> 00:12:40.760
And I, I just want to highlight the concept of the lethal trifecta that Simon Willison has been writing about.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:53.119
And so the idea is that if you have access to, uh, access to private data, exposure to untrusted content, and the ability to communicate out there, an attacker can access your private data and send it back out.

00:12:53.559 --> 00:12:54.679
This is relevant for MCP.

00:12:54.719 --> 00:13:03.359
It's also relevant for, uh, command line utilities, but, uh, there were a lot of cases of exploits using MCP servers, uh, which is what kind of why I raised this here.

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:08.599
There, the MCP server, it's not just that the MCP server would be malicious.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:14.679
It's that by the whole point of adding tools like this is it allows you to access additional data.

00:13:14.719 --> 00:13:17.320
And some of that might be from untrusted sources.

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:23.760
So thinking of GitHub, if I can read issues in public repos, that's access to untrusted content.

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:30.799
Someone could create a malicious GitHub issue with a prompt injection, read the contents of the secret file and do something with that.

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:36.640
And so if I have access to read GitHub issues and write GitHub issues, I have just created this lethal trifecta.

00:13:36.919 --> 00:13:43.719
So this gets to what I think are of some best practices in building, working with MCP servers and in building your own.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:46.799
And I started the talk MCP, but not MCP.

00:13:46.799 --> 00:13:53.359
I am not delivering this talk in iambic pentameter, but I do think it's worth at least one Hamlet reference.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:58.320
In general, brevity is the soul of wit when working and building MCP servers.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:02.000
So the first thing to do is to only define the tools that you need.

00:14:02.119 --> 00:14:11.239
If just with the GitHub example, I said, if we're talking about having access to read issues and write issues being basically enough to get you to the lethal trifecta.

00:14:12.039 --> 00:14:20.119
If I don't have, if I don't have the ability to write, but I don't have the ability to create issues to communicate out only read, well, I've cut off one of those pieces of the trifecta.

00:14:20.799 --> 00:14:25.799
So the security reason is one thing to be, one reason to be thrifty with the tools you define.

00:14:25.799 --> 00:14:32.760
But the other is efficiency, because if all of the tools that I define are taking up context, I don't want to consume extra context.

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:34.359
I don't want to waste tokens.

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:37.599
I don't want to distract from the intent of what I'm trying to do.

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:43.960
So just choosing only the tools that you need and putting them in your MCP server can make it a lot more efficient and more secure.

00:14:44.119 --> 00:14:46.440
Likewise, keeping your responses concise.

00:14:46.479 --> 00:14:56.159
A typical REST API for a product, and certainly that is true with Zendesk, it's going to include a lot of details in, you know, a record that you pull from.

00:14:56.159 --> 00:15:01.599
It might be enough, might be everything that you need to render the webpage view of this, but it's not what the model needs.

00:15:01.599 --> 00:15:05.640
It's not what I need to answer my question of what did the customer say was going wrong?

00:15:06.119 --> 00:15:16.200
I don't need to know, as is the case in the Zendesk API, what the user agent string was of the person when they went to the Zendesk portal and typed in the thing.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:17.799
I just need the content of the text.

00:15:18.239 --> 00:15:23.520
And so stripping these out saves you tokens and makes your, your inference more efficient.

00:15:23.679 --> 00:15:27.799
And likewise, you know, be mindful about your personal data.

00:15:27.799 --> 00:15:31.919
That's my email right there, but I didn't, I have not exposed my, my email there.

